Accurate and calibrated State-of-Health (SOH) estimation is essential for ensuring operational safety, optimizing fast-charging schedules, and enabling dependable second-life reuse of lithium-ion batteries in electrified transportation and grid storage. However, contemporary prognostic paradigms encounter an unresolved trilemma between predictive point accuracy, epistemic uncertainty calibration, and physical interpretability. Purely sub-symbolic deep neural networks act as opaque black boxes prone to severe miscalibration and overconfident extrapolations under cell-to-cell distributional shifts. In contrast, standard Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) provides elegant closed-form Bayesian uncertainty but suffers from cubic $\mathcal{O}(N^3)$ computational bottlenecks and stationary covariance limitations. To resolve this trilemma, we present \textbf{WD-GPR-SR}, a hybrid prognostics architecture unifying Wide-and-Deep Sparse Gaussian Process Regression with Uncertainty-Weighted Symbolic Residual Correction. The framework decomposes degradation modeling into two synergistic tiers: (1) a sparse variational Gaussian process ($M=64$ inducing points) governed by a composite Wide-and-Deep covariance kernel that concurrently captures macroscopic linear aging gradients and non-linear latent manifold interactions with $\mathcal{O}(NM^2)$ computational complexity; and (2) an uncertainty-weighted symbolic regression engine that distills closed-form mathematical corrections from out-of-fold GP residuals, weighted inversely by predictive standard deviation ($w_i \propto 1/(\sigma_i + \epsilon)$) to prevent equation distortion from noisy or high-uncertainty operating points. Evaluated under a strict, leak-free 47-fold Leave-One-Cell-Out (LOCO) cross-validation protocol comprising 6,124 discharge cycles across 47 battery cells from NASA Ames PCoE and CALCE repositories, WD-GPR-SR achieves a mean Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) of $4.854\% \pm 0.495\%$ (peak seed RMSE $4.406\%$), Mean Absolute Error (MAE) of $3.273\% \pm 0.205\%$, and an $R^2$ of $0.9246 \pm 0.0155$, outperforming 14 baseline models including deep neural networks, recurrent networks, gradient boosting trees, and Gaussian processes. Crucially, unlike deterministic baselines that experience extreme interval breaches, WD-GPR-SR delivers calibrated uncertainty intervals with a $95\%$ Prediction Interval Coverage Probability (PICP) of $96.59\% \pm 2.08\%$ and a Negative Log-Likelihood (NLL) of $3.074 \pm 0.073$, alongside an analytical C-compilable symbolic correction term. Finally, we formulate an edge-native compilation pipeline targeting embedded Battery Management System (BMS) architectures, translating neural extractors to TensorRT FP16 engines and compiling symbolic corrections into native C shared libraries.
